# Bug: a keyed read that names every field of an imported record passes
# `kanso check` and then fails when it runs.
#
# Outside the module that declares `pair`, the positional form
# `pair/pair l r = p` is refused (error[opacity]: its structure does not
# cross an import), so the keyed read is the only way left to take both
# fields in one statement. `kanso check` says ok. Running it says:
#   error[runtime]: a keyed read omits at least one field; reading every
#   field is the positional form
#
# The same happens inside the declaring module, where `{ left right } = p`
# also passes check and fails at run time. In cli/cli.kso a seven-field
# keyed read of a local record instead produced "unknown name" errors for
# every field it bound.
#
# Expected: either the read is allowed (it is the only one-statement read an
# importer has) or `kanso check` refuses it with that message.
import "./pair"

{ left right } = pair/make 1 2
print "{left} {right}"
